helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Number Format Codes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153514				0	gu	\<variable id=\"zahlenformatcodes\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05020301.xhp\" name=\"Number Format Codes\"\>Number Format Codes\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150467				0	gu	Number format codes can consist of up to three sections separated by a semicolon (;).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150146				0	gu	In a number format code with two sections, the first section applies to positive values and zero, and the second section applies to negative values.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3158442				0	gu	In a number format code with three sections, the first section applies to positive values, the second section to negative values, and the third section to the value zero.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155069				0	gu	You can also assign conditions to the three sections, so that the format is only applied if a condition is met.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151262				0	gu	Decimal Places and Significant Digits				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153624	3			0	gu	Use zero (0) or the number sign (#) as placeholders in your number format code to represent numbers. The (#) only displays significant digits, while the (0) displays zeroes if there are fewer digits in the number than in the number format.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153323				0	gu	Use question marks (?) to represent the number of digits to include in the numerator and the denominator of a fraction. Fractions that do not fit the pattern that you define are displayed as floating point numbers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148440				0	gu	If a number contains more digits to the right of the decimal delimiter than there are placeholders in the format, the number is rounded accordingly. If a number contains more digits to the left of the decimal delimiter than there are placeholders in the format, the entire number is displayed. Use the following list as a guide for using placeholders when you create a number format code:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150902				0	gu	Placeholders				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3157896				0	gu	Explanation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152801				0	gu	#				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3145090				0	gu	Does not display extra zeros.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147088				0	gu	0 (Zero)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150774				0	gu	Displays extra zeros if the number has less places than zeros in the format.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_idN1087E				0	gu	Examples				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154749				0	gu	Format code				2002-02-02 02:02:02
