helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156039				0	gu	To display seconds as fractions, add the decimal delimiter to your number format code. For example, enter \<emph\>hh:mm:ss.00\</emph\> to display the time as "01:02:03.45".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148649				0	gu	If a time is entered in the form 02:03.45 or 01:02:03.45 or 25:01:02, the following formats are assigned if no other time format has been specified: MM:SS.00 or [HH]:MM:SS.00 or [HH]:MM:SS				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158404				0	gu	Displaying Numbers Using Native Characters				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149998				0	gu	To display numbers using native number characters, use a [NatNum1], [NatNum2], ... [NatNum11] modifier at the beginning of a number format codes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154600				0	gu	The [NatNum1] modifier always uses a one to one character mapping to convert numbers to a string that matches the native number format code of the corresponding locale. The other modifiers produce different results if they are used with different locales. A locale can be the language and the territory for which the format code is defined, or a modifier such as [$-yyy] that follows the native number modifier. In this case, yyy is the hexadecimal MS-LCID that is also used in currency format codes. For example, to display a number using Japanese short Kanji characters in an English US locale, use the following number format code:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152546				0	gu	[NatNum1][$-411]0				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147269				0	gu	In the following list, the Microsoft Excel [DBNumX] modifier that corresponds to \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> [NatNum] modifier is shown. If you want, you can use a [DBNumX] modifier instead of [NatNum] modifier for your locale. Whenever possible, \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> internally maps [DBNumX] modifiers to [NatNumN] modifiers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_idN11234				0	gu	Displaying dates using [NatNum] modifiers can have a different effect than displaying other types of numbers. Such effects are indicated by 'CAL: '. For example, 'CAL: 1/4/4' indicates that the year is displayed using the [NatNum1] modifier, while the day and month are displayed using the [NatNum4] modifier. If 'CAL' is not specified, the date formats for that particular modifier are not supported.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153111				0	gu	[NatNum1] Transliterations				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3146895				0	gu	Chinese: Chinese lower case characters; CAL: 1/7/7 [DBNum1]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152536				0	gu	Japanese: short Kanji characters [DBNum1]; CAL: 1/4/4 [DBNum1]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\05020301.xhp	0	help	par_id3146125				0	gu	Korean: Korean lower case characters [DBNum1]; CAL: 1/7/7 [DBNum1]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
