helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147543				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>measurement units; converting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>units; converting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>converting;metrics\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>metrics;converting\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_idN106AD				0	gu	In input boxes for length units you can also add the unit abbreviation according to the following list:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_idN106BA				0	gu	Unit abbreviation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_idN106C0				0	gu	Explanation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_idN106E1				0	gu	in or "				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_idN106EE				0	gu	pi				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_idN10704				0	gu	The following formulas convert the units:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_idN1070A				0	gu	1 cm = 10 mm				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_idN1070E				0	gu	1 inch = 2.54 cm				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_idN10712				0	gu	1 inch = 6 Pica = 72 Point				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_idN10715				0	gu	For example, in a text document, open \<emph\>Format - Paragraph - Indents & Spacing\</emph\>. To indent the current paragraph by one inch, enter \<item type=\"literal\"\>1 in\</item\> or \<item type=\"literal\"\>1"\</item\> into the "Before text" box. To indent the paragraph by 1 cm, enter \<item type=\"literal\"\>1 cm\</item\> into the input box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_idN1074C				0	gu	To input the maximum or minimum allowed value respectively, click the current value and then press the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Page Up\</item\> or \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Page Down\</item\> key.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	par_id3153070				0	gu	You can specify which options, such as basic font, language, character set, or break, are imported or exported with a text document. The dialog appears when you load an ASCII file with the filter "Text Encoded" or when you save the document the first time, or when you "save as" with another name.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	par_id3155577				0	gu	Defines the settings for importing or exporting your file. When exporting, only the character set and paragraph break can be defined.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146959				0	gu	Character Set				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	par_id3143206				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"SW:LISTBOX:DLG_ASCII_FILTER:LB_CHARSET\"\>Specifies the character set of the file for export or import.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154926				0	gu	મૂળભુત ફોન્ટ				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	par_id3153323				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"SW:LISTBOX:DLG_ASCII_FILTER:LB_LANGUAGE\"\>Specifies the language of the text, if this has not already been defined. This setting is only available when importing.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147143				0	gu	Paragraph break				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	par_id3143281				0	gu	Defines the type of paragraph break for a text line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150935				0	gu	CR & LF				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	par_id3145829				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"SW:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_ASCII_FILTER:RB_CRLF\"\>Produces a "Carriage Return" and a "Linefeed". This option is the default.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153551				0	gu	CR				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	par_id3156042				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"SW:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_ASCII_FILTER:RB_CR\"\>Produces a "Carriage Return" as the paragraph break.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150713				0	gu	LF				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000215.xhp	0	help	par_id3145090				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"SW:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_ASCII_FILTER:RB_LF\"\>Produces a "Linefeed" as the paragraph break.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	XML File Formats				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154408				0	gu	\<variable id=\"xmlformat\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000021.xhp\" name=\"XML File Formats\"\>XML File Formats\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3148919				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DID_SAVE_PACKED_XML\"\>By default, $[officename] loads and saves files in the OpenDocument file format.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156324				0	gu	OpenDocument file format names				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3154926				0	gu	%PRODUCTNAME %PRODUCTVERSION uses the following file formats:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3157898				0	gu	Document format				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN10767				0	gu	*.odt				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN10772				0	gu	*.ott				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN1077D				0	gu	*.odm				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN10783				0	gu	HTML દસ્તાવેજ				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN10788				0	gu	*.html				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN1078E				0	gu	HTML Document Template				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN10793				0	gu	*.oth				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN1079E				0	gu	*.ods				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A9				0	gu	*.ots				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN107B4				0	gu	*.odg				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN107BF				0	gu	*.otg				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN107CA				0	gu	*.odp				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN107D5				0	gu	*.otp				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN107E0				0	gu	*.odf				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN10860				0	gu	*.odb				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id2089907				0	gu	*.odt				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN1088F				0	gu	The HTML format is not an OpenDocument format.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN107E3				0	gu	Older File Formats Prior to %PRODUCTNAME %PRODUCTVERSION				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150398				0	gu	XML file structure				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3149649				0	gu	Documents in OpenDocument file format are stored as compressed zip archives that contain XML files. To view these XML files, you can open the OpenDocument file with an unzip program. The following files and directories are contained within the OpenDocument files:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3153178				0	gu	The text content of the document is located in \<emph\>content.xml\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3145152				0	gu	The file \<emph\>meta.xml\</emph\> contains the meta information of the document, which you can enter under \<emph\>File - Properties\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3150740	33			0	gu	If you save a document with a password, \<emph\>meta.xml\</emph\> will not be encrypted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391				0	gu	The file \<emph\>settings.xml\</emph\> contains further information about the settings for this document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3150447	35			0	gu	In \<emph\>styles.xml,\</emph\> you find the styles applied to the document that can be seen in the Styles and Formatting window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3153353	36			0	gu	The \<emph\>meta-inf/manifest.xml\</emph\> file describes the structure of the XML file.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	gu	Additional files and folders can be contained in the packed file format.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154299				0	gu	Definition of the XML formats				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AAD				0	gu	The schema for the OpenDocument formats can be found on the \<link href=\"http://www.oasis-open.org\"\>www.oasis-open.org\</link\> web site.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_id3152596				0	gu	Note that the formats are subject to certain licenses. You can find notes on the licenses at the start of the DTD files. More detailed information can be found on the \<link href=\"http://www.openoffice.org\" name=\"OpenOffice.org\"\>OpenOffice.org\</link\> website.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000021.xhp	0	help	par_idN10AC5				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01130000.xhp\"\>Document Converter Wizard\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000409.xhp	0	help	par_id3156053				0	gu	Choose \<emph\>Data - Filter - Standard Filter\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000409.xhp	0	help	par_id3154350				0	gu	Database table view: \<emph\>Standard Filter\</emph\> icon in the \<emph\>Database\</emph\> Toolbar				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000409.xhp	0	help	par_id3154183				0	gu	Form view: \<emph\>Standard Filter\</emph\> icon in the \<emph\>Form\</emph\> Bar				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000409.xhp	0	help	par_id3155619				0	gu	\<image src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_formfiltered.png\" id=\"img_id3147588\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3147588\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000409.xhp	0	help	par_id3148731				0	gu	પ્રમાણભૂત ગાળક				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000407.xhp	0	help	par_id3083278				0	gu	\<variable id=\"window\"\>Choose \<emph\>Window - New Window\</emph\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000407.xhp	0	help	par_id3154545				0	gu	\<variable id=\"liste\"\>Choose \<emph\>Window\</emph\> - List of open documents\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Glossary of Internet Terms				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150702				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>Internet glossary\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>common terms;Internet glossary\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>glossaries;Internet terms\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>terminology;Internet glossary\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150702				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp\" name=\"Glossary of Internet Terms\"\>Glossary of Internet Terms\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3155577				0	gu	If you are a newcomer to the Internet, you will be confronted with unfamiliar terms: browser, bookmark, e-mail, homepage, search engine, and many others. To make your first steps easier, this glossary explains some of the more important terminology you may find in the Internet, intranet, mail and news.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153146				0	gu	ચોકઠું				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3157909				0	gu	Frames are useful for designing the layout of\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#html\" name=\"HTML\"\>HTML\</link\> pages. $[officename] uses floating frames into which you can place objects such as graphics, movie files and sound. The context menu of a frame shows the options for restoring or editing frame contents. Some of these commands are also listed in \<emph\>Edit - Object\</emph\> when the frame is selected.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147077				0	gu	FTP				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3147335				0	gu	FTP stands for File Transfer Protocol and is the standard transfer protocol for files in the Internet. An FTP server is a program on a computer connected to the Internet which stores files to be transmitted with the aid of FTP. While FTP is responsible for transmitting and downloading Internet files, \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#http\" name=\"HTTP\"\>HTTP\</link\> (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) provides the connection setup and data transfer between WWW servers and clients.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145609				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>HTML; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145609				0	gu	HTML				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3161459				0	gu	HTML (Hypertext Markup Language) is a document code language, which is used as the file format for WWW documents. It is derived from \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#sgml\" name=\"SGML\"\>SGML\</link\> and integrates text, graphics, videos and sound.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154346	58			0	gu	If you want to type HTML commands directly, for example when doing exercises from one of the many available HTML books, remember that HTML pages are pure text files. Save your document under the document type \<emph\>Text \</emph\>and give it the file name extension .HTM. Be sure there are no umlauts or other special characters of the extended character set. If you want to re-open this file in $[officename] and edit the HTML code, you must load it with the file type \<emph\>Text\</emph\> and not with the file type \<emph\>Web pages\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153960				0	gu	There are several references on the Internet providing an introduction to the HTML language.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147423				0	gu	HTTP				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153379				0	gu	The Hypertext Transfer Protocol is a record of transmission of WWW documents between WWW servers (hosts) and browsers (clients).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149290				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>hyperlinks; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149290				0	gu	હાઇપરલિંક				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3145420				0	gu	Hyperlinks are cross-references, highlighted in text in various colors and activated by mouse-click. With the aid of hyperlinks, readers can jump to specific information within a document as well as to related information in other documents.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3156281				0	gu	In $[officename] you can assign hyperlinks to text as well as to graphics and text frames (see the Hyperlink Dialog icon on the Standard bar).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152805				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>ImageMap; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152805				0	gu	ચિત્ર-નક્શો				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154685				0	gu	An ImageMap is a reference-sensitive graphic or text frame. You can click on defined areas of the graphic or text frame to go to a target (\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#url\" name=\"URL\"\>URL\</link\>), which is linked with the area. The reference areas, along with the linked URLs and corresponding text displayed when resting the mouse pointer on these areas, are defined in the \<link href=\"text/shared/01/02220000.xhp\" name=\"ImageMap Editor\"\>ImageMap Editor\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153178				0	gu	There are two different types of ImageMaps. A Client Side ImageMap is evaluated on the client computer, which loaded the graphic from the Internet, while a Server Side ImageMap is evaluated on the server computer which provides the \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#html\" name=\"HTML\"\>HTML\</link\> page on the Internet. In server evaluation, clicking an ImageMap sends the relative coordinates of the cursor within the image to the server, and a dedicated program on the server responds. In the client evaluation, clicking a defined hotspot of the ImageMap activates the URL, as if it were a normal text link. The URL appears below the mouse pointer when passing across the ImageMap.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3150740				0	gu	As ImageMaps can be used in different ways, they can be stored in different formats.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146874				0	gu	ImageMap Formats				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3145153				0	gu	ImageMaps are basically divided between those that are analyzed on the server (i. e. your Internet provider) and those analyzed on the web browser of the reader's computer.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152881				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>Server Side ImageMap\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152881				0	gu	Server Side ImageMaps				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153057				0	gu	Server Side ImageMaps appear for the reader as a picture or frame on the page. Click on the ImageMap with the mouse, and the coordinates of the relative position are sent to the server. Aided by an extra program, the server then determines the next step to take. There are several incompatible methods to define this process, the two most common being:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3147502				0	gu	W3C (CERN) HTTP Server (Format type: MAP - CERN)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154011				0	gu	NCSA HTTP Server (Format type: MAP - NCSA)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3149483	74			0	gu	$[officename] creates ImageMaps for both methods. Select the format from the \<emph\>Save as type \</emph\>list in the \<emph\>Save As \</emph\>dialog in the \<emph\>ImageMap Editor\</emph\>. Separate Map Files are created which you must upload to the server. You will need to ask your provider or network administrator which type of ImageMaps are supported by the server and how to access the evaluation program.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152418				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>Client Side ImageMap\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152418				0	gu	Client Side ImageMap				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664				0	gu	When saving the ImageMap, select the file type \<emph\>SIP - StarView ImageMap\</emph\>. This saves the ImageMap directly in a format which can be applied to every active picture or frame in your document. However, if you just want to use the ImageMap on the current picture or text frame, you do not have to save it in any special format. After defining the regions, simply click \<emph\>Apply\</emph\>. Nothing more is necessary. Client Side ImageMaps saved in \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#html\" name=\"HTML\"\>HTML\</link\> format are inserted directly into the page in HTML code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159125				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>Java; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159125				0	gu	જાવા				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159153				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>plug-ins; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159153				0	gu	Plug-In				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154127				0	gu	Extensions providing additional functionality in Web browsers are referred to as Plug-Ins.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3147484				0	gu	A Plug-In is a term used in various contexts:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3168608				0	gu	Plug-Ins in $[officename]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148387				0	gu	Using Plug-Ins to extend your programs				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3156737				0	gu	Plug-ins, generally speaking, are software additions to particular applications which provide enhanced functionality. Often import and export filters for various file formats are stored as plug-ins in a plug-in directory.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3149958				0	gu	Netscape web browser extensions produced by Netscape Communication Corporation are also called plug-ins. These are external programs mainly taken from the multimedia field and which communicate with the browser through standardized interfaces. These plug-ins can be linked to $[officename] documents.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145647				0	gu	પ્રોક્સી				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3148455				0	gu	A proxy is a computer in the network acting as a kind of clipboard for data transfer. Whenever you access the Internet from a company network and request a Web page that has already been read by a colleague, the proxy will be able to display the page much quicker, as long as it's still in the memory. All that has to be checked in this case is that the page stored in the proxy is the latest version. If this is the case, the page won't have to be downloaded from the much slower Internet but can be loaded directly from the proxy.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154729				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>SGML; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154729				0	gu	SGML				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3147330				0	gu	SGML stands for "Standard Generalized Markup Language". SGML is based on the idea that documents have structural and other semantic elements that can be described without reference to how such elements should be displayed. The actual display of such a document may vary, depending on the output medium and style preferences. In structured texts, SGML not only defines structures (in the DTD = Document Type Definition) but also ensures they are consistently used.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3148747				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#html\" name=\"HTML\"\>HTML\</link\> is a specialized application of SGML. This means that most Web browsers support only a limited range of SGML standards and that almost all SGML-enabled systems can produce attractive HTML pages.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153950				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>search engines; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153950				0	gu	Search Engines				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3157965				0	gu	A search engine is a service in the Internet based on a software program used to explore a vast amount of information using key words.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150751				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>tags; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150751				0	gu	Tags				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3156360				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#html\" name=\"HTML\"\>HTML\</link\> pages contain certain structural and formatting instructions called tags. Tags are code words enclosed by brackets in the document description language HTML. Many tags contain text or hyperlink references between the opening and closing brackets. For example, titles are marked by the tags <h1> at the beginning and </h1> at the end of the title. Some tags only appear on their own such as <br> for a line break or <img ...> to link a graphic.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153766				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>URL; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153766				0	gu	URL				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3152931				0	gu	The Uniform Resource Locator (URL) displays the address of a document or a server in the Internet. The general structure of a URL varies according to type and is generally in the form Service://Hostname:Port/Path/Page#Mark although not all elements are always required. An URL can be a FTP address, a WWW (HTTP) address, a file address or an e-mail address.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	General Glossary				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154896				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000005.xhp\" name=\"General Glossary\"\>General Glossary\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154788				0	gu	This glossary includes explanations of some of the most important terms you will come across in $[officename].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154873				0	gu	Use the glossary to look up unfamiliar terms found in any $[officename] application.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156192				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>ASCII; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156192				0	gu	ASCII				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3155922				0	gu	Abbreviation for American Standard Code for Information Interchange. ASCII is a character set for displaying fonts on personal computers. It consists of 128 characters including letters, numbers, punctuation and symbols. The extended ASCII character set contains 256 characters. Each character has been assigned a unique number, also referred to as ASCII Code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151245				0	gu	Bézier Object				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154924				0	gu	Developed by the French mathematician Pierre Bézier, a Bézier curve is a mathematically defined curve used in two-dimensional graphic applications. The curve is defined by four points: the initial position and the terminating position, and two separate middle points. Bézier objects can be modified by moving these points with the mouse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146907				0	gu	Complex Text Layout (CTL)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3156081				0	gu	Languages with complex text layout may have some or all of the following features:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3145116				0	gu	The language is written with characters or glyphs that are composed of several parts				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154630				0	gu	The text direction is from right to left.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3148677				0	gu	Currently, $[officename] supports Hindi, Thai, Hebrew, and Arabic as CTL languages.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147084				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>DDE; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147084				0	gu	DDE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3145154				0	gu	DDE stands for "Dynamic Data Exchange," which is a predecessor of OLE, "Object Linking and Embedding". With DDE, objects are linked through file reference, but not embedded.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154820				0	gu	You can create a DDE link using the following procedure: Select cells from a Calc spreadsheet, copy them into the clipboard and switch to another spreadsheet and select the \<emph\>Edit - Paste Special\</emph\> dialog. Select \<emph\>the Link\</emph\> option to insert the contents as a DDE link. When activating a link, the inserted cell area will be read from its original file.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150439				0	gu	Direct and Style Formatting				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3159254				0	gu	If you format a document without Styles, it is referred to as "direct" formatting. This means modifying text or other objects, such as frames or tables, by applying various attributes directly. The format applies only to the selected area and all changes must be made separately. Styles, on the other hand, are not applied to the text directly, but rather are defined in the Styles and Formatting window and then applied. One advantage is that when you change a Style, all parts of the document to which that Style is assigned are modified at the same time.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3147287				0	gu	You can remove direct formatting from your document by selecting the entire text with the shortcut keys \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+A and then choosing \<emph\>Format - Default Formatting\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155132				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>windows; docking definition\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>docking; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155132				0	gu	Docking				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154638				0	gu	\<variable id=\"andock1\"\>Some windows in $[officename], for example the Styles and Formatting window and the Navigator, are "dockable" windows. You can move these windows, re-size them or dock them to an edge. On each edge you can dock several windows on top of, or alongside each other; then, by moving the border lines, you can change the relative proportions of the windows. \</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155306				0	gu	Docking (AutoHide)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3155854				0	gu	On any window edge where another window is docked you will see a button which allows you to show or hide the window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3143274				0	gu	If you click the button on the window edge to show the window, the window will remain visible until you manually hide it again (with the same button).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3153093				0	gu	If you show the window by clicking the window border, but not the button, you activate the \<emph\>AutoHide\</emph\> function. The AutoHide function allows you to temporarily show a hidden window by clicking on its edge. When you click in the document, the docked window hides again.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3163710				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>formatting; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163710				0	gu	ફોર્મેટીંગ				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3163821				0	gu	Formatting refers to the visual layout of text using a word-processing or DTP program. This includes defining the paper format, page borders, fonts and font effects, as well as indents and spacing. You can format text \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000005.xhp#Section7\"\>directly or with Styles\</link\> provided by $[officename].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156006				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>IME; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156006				0	gu	IME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3157874				0	gu	IME stands for Input Method Editor. A program that allows the user to enter complex characters from non-western character sets using a standard keyboard.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151172				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>JDBC; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151172				0	gu	JDBC				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3148386				0	gu	You can use the Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) API to connect to a database from %PRODUCTNAME. JDBC drivers are written in the Java programming language and are platform independent.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151282				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>kerning; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151282				0	gu	કરનિંગ				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3146321				0	gu	Kerning means increasing or decreasing the amount of space between pairs of letters to improve the overall appearance of the text.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3146078				0	gu	The kerning tables contain information on which pairs of letters require more spacing. These tables are generally a component of a font.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150592				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>links; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150592				0	gu	Link				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3144765				0	gu	If you insert the object as a link, only a reference to the file name is inserted. The file size of the document increases only by the path and file reference. If you open your document on another computer, however, the linked file must be in exactly the same position as given by the reference in order to view the object in the document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154512				0	gu	Number System				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156358				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>objects; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156358				0	gu	વસ્તુ				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3144748				0	gu	An object is a screen element containing data. It can refer to application data, such as text or graphics.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3153839				0	gu	Objects are independent and do not influence each other. Any object containing data can be assigned certain commands. For example, a graphic object has commands for image editing and a spreadsheet contains calculation commands.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152827				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>ODBC; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152827				0	gu	ODBC				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3153530				0	gu	Open Database Connectivity (ODBC) is a protocol norm with which applications can access database systems. The query language used is Structured Query Language (SQL). In $[officename], you can determine for each database whether to use SQL commands to run queries. Alternatively, you can use the interactive help to define your query by mouseclick and have it automatically translated into SQL by $[officename].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3153956				0	gu	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"WIN\"\>The 32bit ODBC functions required here can be installed on your system at any time with the help of the setup program supplied with your database. You can then amend the properties through the Control Panel. \</caseinline\>\</switchinline\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154479				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>OLE; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154479				0	gu	OLE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3157840				0	gu	Object Linking and Embedding (OLE) objects can be linked to a target document or may also be embedded. Embedding inserts a copy of the object and details of the source program in the target document. If you want to edit the object, simply activate the source program by double-clicking on the object.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154507				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>OpenGL; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154507				0	gu	OpenGL				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3146879				0	gu	OpenGL represents a 3D graphics language, initially developed by SGI (Silicon Graphics Inc). Two dialects of this language are commonly used: Microsoft OpenGL, developed for use under Windows NT, and Cosmo OpenGL made by SGI. The latter represents an independent graphics language for all platforms and all kind of computers, even usable on machines without special 3-D graphics hardware.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155764				0	gu	PNG				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3083286				0	gu	Primary key				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3150323				0	gu	A primary key serves as a unique identifier of database fields. The unique identification of database fields is used in \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000005.xhp#relational\"\>relational databases\</link\>, to access data in other tables. If reference is made to a primary key from another table, this is termed a foreign key.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147359				0	gu	Relational Database				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3147585	101			0	gu	A relational database is a collection of data items organized as a set of formally described tables from which data can be accessed or reassembled in many different ways without having to reorganize the database tables. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154255				0	gu	A relational database management system (RDBMS) is a program that lets you create, update, and administer a relational database. An RDBMS takes Structured Query Language (SQL) statements entered by a user or contained in an application program and creates, updates, or provides access to the database.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147315				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>register-true; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147315				0	gu	નોંધણી-સાચી				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3154223				0	gu	Register-true is a typography term that is used in printing. This term refers to the congruent imprint of the lines within a type area on the front and the back side of book pages, newspaper pages and magazine pages. The register-true feature make these pages easier to read by preventing gray shadows from shining through between the lines of text. The register-true term also refers to lines in adjacent text columns that are of the same height.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3145230				0	gu	When you define a paragraph, Paragraph Style, or a Page Style as register-true, the base lines of the affected characters are aligned to a vertical page grid, regardless of font size or of the presence of graphics. If you want, you can specify the setting for this grid as a Page Style property.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156710				0	gu	RTF				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3151186				0	gu	Rich Text Format (RTF) is a file format developed for the exchange of text files. A special feature is that the formatting is converted into directly readable text information. Unfortunately, in comparison to other file formats, this creates relatively large files.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156372				0	gu	Saving Relatively and Absolutely				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3146919				0	gu	In various dialogs (for example, \<emph\>Edit - AutoText\</emph\>) you can select whether you want to save files relatively or absolutely.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3152946				0	gu	If you choose to save relatively, the references to embedded graphics or other objects in your document will be saved relative to the location in the file system. In this case, it does not matter where the referenced directory structure is recorded. The files will be found regardless of location, as long as the reference remains on the same drive or volume. This is important if you want to make the document available to other computers that may have a completely different directory structure, drive or volume names. It is also recommended to save relatively if you want to create a directory structure on an Internet server.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3148927				0	gu	If you prefer absolute saving, all references to other files will also be defined as absolute, based on the respective drive, volume or root directory. The advantage is that the document containing the references can be moved to other directories or folders, and the references remain valid.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149922				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>SQL;definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149922				0	gu	SQL				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3152863				0	gu	Structured Query Language (SQL) is a language used for database queries. In $[officename] you can formulate queries either in SQL or interactively with the mouse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147552				0	gu	SQL Database / SQL Server				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3159239				0	gu	An SQL database is a database system which offers an \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000005.xhp#sql\"\>SQL\</link\> interface. SQL databases are often used in client/server networks in which different clients access a central server (for example, an SQL server), hence they are also called SQL server databases, or SQL servers for short.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166423				0	gu	Widows and Orphans				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\00\00000005.xhp	0	help	par_id3149448				0	gu	Widows and orphans are historical typography terms, which have been in use for many years. A widow refers to a short line at the end of a paragraph, which when printed, appears alone at the top of the next page. An orphan is, in contrast, the first line of a paragraph printed alone at the bottom of the previous page. In a $[officename] text document you can automatically prevent such occurrences in the desired Paragraph Style. When doing so, you can determine the minimum amount of lines to be kept together on a page.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
