helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3163625				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_SUMME\"\>Adds all the numbers in a range of cells.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3163656				0	gu	SUM(number1; number 2; ...; number 30)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3163671				0	gu	\<emph\>Number 1 to number 30\</emph\> are up to 30 arguments whose sum is to be calculated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163690				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3151756				0	gu	Conditions linked by AND can be used with the function SUM() in the following manner:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3151774				0	gu	Example assumption: You have entered invoices into a table. Column A contains the date value of the invoice, column B the amounts. You want to find a formula that you can use to return the total of all amounts only for a specific month, e.g. only the amount for the period >=1.1.99 to <1.2.99. The range with the date values covers A1:A40, the range containing the amounts to be totaled is B1:B40. C1 contains the start date, 1.1.99, of the invoices to be included and C2 the date, 1.2.99, that is no longer included.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3151799				0	gu	Enter the following formula as an array formula:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3151828				0	gu	In order to enter this as an array formula, you must press the Shift \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>+ Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> + Enter key instead of simply pressing the Enter key to close the formula. The formula will then be shown in the \<emph\>Formula\</emph\> bar enclosed in braces.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3151869				0	gu	{=SUM((A1:A40>=C1)*(A1:A40<C2)*B1:B40)}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151957				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>EXPONDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponential distributions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151957				0	gu	SUMIF				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3151986				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_SUMMEWENN\"\>Adds the cells specified by a given criteria.\</ahelp\> This function is used to browse a range when you search for a certain value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3152028				0	gu	SUMIF(range; criteria; sum_range)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3152062				0	gu	\<emph\>Criteria\</emph\> is the cell in which the search criterion is shown, or the search criterion itself. If the criteria is written into the formula, it has to be surrounded by double quotes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3152083				0	gu	\<emph\>Sum_range\</emph\> is the range from which values are summed. If this parameter has not been indicated, the values found in the \<emph\>Range\</emph\> are summed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
