helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3165000				0	gu	\<emph\>Criteria\</emph\> indicates the criteria in the form of a number, an expression or a character string. These criteria determine which cells are counted. You may also enter a search text in the form of a regular expression, e.g. "b.*" for all words that begin with b. You may also indicate a cell range that contains the search criterion. If you search for literal text, enclose the text in double quotes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3165037				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_today.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	TODAY				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_today.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145659				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>TODAY function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_today.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145659				0	gu	\<variable id=\"today\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_today.xhp\"\>TODAY\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_today.xhp	0	help	par_id3153759				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_HEUTE\"\>Returns the current computer system date.\</ahelp\> The value is updated when you reopen the document or modify the values of the document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_today.xhp	0	help	par_id3153154				0	gu	TODAY()				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_today.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153627				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days360.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	DAYS360 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days360.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148555				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>DAYS360 function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days360.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148555				0	gu	\<variable id=\"days360\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_days360.xhp\"\>DAYS360\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days360.xhp	0	help	par_id3156032				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_TAGE360\"\>Returns the difference between two dates based on the 360 day year used in interest calculations.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days360.xhp	0	help	par_id3151064				0	gu	The optional argument \<emph\>Type\</emph\> determines the type of difference calculation. If Type = 0 or if the argument is missing, the US method (NASD, National Association of Securities Dealers) is used. If Type <> 0, the European method is used.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days360.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148641				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days360.xhp	0	help	par_id3156348				0	gu	DAYS360("1/1/2000";NOW()) returns the number of interest days from January 1, 2000 until today.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_datevalue.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	DATEVALUE 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_datevalue.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145621				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>DATEVALUE function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_datevalue.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145621				0	gu	\<variable id=\"datevalue\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_datevalue.xhp\"\>DATEVALUE\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_datevalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3145087				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DATWERT\"\>Returns the internal date number for text in quotes.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_datevalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3149281				0	gu	The internal date number is returned as a number. The number is determined by the date system that is used by $[officename] to calculate dates.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_datevalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3149268				0	gu	DATEVALUE("Text")				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_datevalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3154819	23			0	gu	\<emph\>Text\</emph\> is a valid date expression and must be entered with quotation marks.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_datevalue.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156309				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3145173	2			0	gu	\<variable id=\"datenbanktext\"\>This section deals with functions used with data organized as one row of data for one record. \</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154016				0	gu	The \<emph\>Database\</emph\> category may be confused with a database integrated in $[officename]. However, there is no connection between a database in $[officename] and the \<emph\>Database\</emph\> category in $[officename] Calc.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150329				0	gu	Example Data:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153713				0	gu	The following data will be used in some of the function description examples:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155766				0	gu	The range A1:E10 lists the children invited to Joe's birthday party. The following information is given for each entry: column A shows the name, B the grade, then age in years, distance to school in meters and weight in kilograms.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149282				0	gu	The formula in cell B16 is =DCOUNT(A1:E10;0;A13:E14)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150962				0	gu	Database Function Parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155837				0	gu	The following are the parameter definitions for all database functions:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149453				0	gu	\<emph\>Database\</emph\> is the cell range defining the database.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3151272	86			0	gu	\<emph\>Database field\</emph\> specifies the column where the function operates on after the search criteria of the first parameter is applied and the data rows are selected. It is not related to the search criteria itself. Use the number 0 to specify the whole data range. \<variable id=\"quotes\"\>To reference a column by means of the column header name, place quotation marks around the header name. \</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3147083				0	gu	\<emph\>Search criteria\</emph\> is the cell range containing search criteria. If you write several criteria in one row they are connected by AND. If you write the criteria in different rows they are connected by OR. Empty cells in the search criteria range will be ignored.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150882				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>EXPONDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponential distributions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150882				0	gu	DCOUNTA				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156133				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBANZAHL\"\>DCOUNT counts the number of rows (records) in a database that match the specified search criteria and contain numerical values.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153218				0	gu	DCOUNT(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153273	187			0	gu	For the Database Field parameter you can enter a cell to specify the column, or enter the number 0 for the entire database. The parameter cannot be empty. \<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/01/04060101.xhp#quotes\"/\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154743				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153623				0	gu	In the example above (scroll up, please), we want to know how many children have to travel more than 600 meters to school. The result is to be stored in cell B16. Set the cursor in cell B16. Enter the formula =DCOUNT(A1:E10;0;A13:E14) in B16. The \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\> helps you to input ranges.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149142				0	gu	\<emph\>Database\</emph\> is the range of data to be evaluated, including its headers: in this case A1:E10. \<emph\>Database field\</emph\> specifies the column for the search criteria: in this case, the whole database. \<emph\>Search criteria\</emph\> is the range where you can enter the search parameters: in this case, A13:E14.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3145652				0	gu	To learn how many children in second grade are over 7 years of age, delete the entry >600 in cell D14 and enter "2" in cell B14 under Grade, and enter >7 in cell C14 to the right. The result is 2. Two children are in second grade and over 7 years of age. As both criteria are in the same row, they are connected by AND.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156123				0	gu	DCOUNTA				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156110				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBANZAHL2\"\>DCOUNTA counts the number of rows (records) in a database that match the specified search conditions, and contain numeric or alphanumeric values.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3146893				0	gu	DCOUNTA(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149751				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147256				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>PV function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>present values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; present values\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147256				0	gu	DGET				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3152801				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBAUSZUG\"\>DGET returns the contents of the referenced cell in a database which matches the specified search criteria.\</ahelp\> In case of an error, the function returns either #VALUE! for no row found, or Err502 for more than one cell found.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154696				0	gu	DGET(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153909				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155388				0	gu	In the above example (scroll up, please), we want to determine what grade a child is in, whose name was entered in cell A14. The formula is entered in cell B16 and differs slightly from the earlier examples because only one column (one database field) can be entered for \<emph\>Database field\</emph\>. Enter the following formula:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148833				0	gu	Or enter the value 11 in cell C14 only, and delete the other entries in this row. Edit the formula in B16 as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148813				0	gu	Instead of the grade, the name is queried. The answer appears at once: Daniel is the only child aged 11.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149766				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>PV function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>present values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; present values\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149766				0	gu	DMAX				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154903				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBMAX\"\>DMAX returns the maximum content of a cell (field) in a database (all records) that matches the specified search conditions.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3159157				0	gu	DMAX(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145420				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148442				0	gu	To find out how much the heaviest child in each grade weighed in the above example (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150510				0	gu	Under 'Grade', enter 1, 2, 3, and so on, one after the other. After entering a grade number, the weight of the heaviest child in that grade appears.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159141				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>PV function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>present values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; present values\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159141				0	gu	DMIN				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154261				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBMIN\"\>DMIN returns the minimum content of a cell (field) in a database that matches the specified search criteria.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148479				0	gu	DMIN(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151050				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148925				0	gu	To find the shortest distance to school for the children in each grade in the above example (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148917				0	gu	In row 14, under Grade, enter 1, 2, 3, and so on, one after the other. The shortest distance to school for each grade appears.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154274				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>PV function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>present values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; present values\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154274				0	gu	DAVERAGE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3166453				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBMITTELWERT\"\>DAVERAGE returns the average of the values of all cells (fields) in all rows (database records) that match the specified search criteria.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150710				0	gu	DAVERAGE(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152943				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149104				0	gu	To find the average weight of all children of the same age in the above example (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155587				0	gu	In row 14, under Age, enter 7, 8, 9, and so on, one after the other. The average weight of all children of the same age appears.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159269				0	gu	DPRODUCT				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3152879				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBPRODUKT\"\>DPRODUCT multiplies all cells of a data range where the cell contents match the search criteria.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154854				0	gu	DPRODUCT(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149802				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148986				0	gu	With the birthday party example above (scroll up, please), there is no meaningful application of this function.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148462				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>EXPONDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponential distributions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148462				0	gu	DSTDEV				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154605				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBSTDABW\"\>DSTDEV calculates the standard deviation of a population based on a sample, using the numbers in a database column that match the given conditions.\</ahelp\> The records are treated as a sample of data. That means that the children in the example represent a cross section of all children. Note that a representative result can not be obtained from a sample of less than one thousand.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148661				0	gu	DSTDEV(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153945				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149934				0	gu	To find the standard deviation of the weight for all children of the same age in the example (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153536				0	gu	In row 14, under Age, enter 7, 8, 9, and so on, one after the other. The result shown is the standard deviation of the weight of all children of this age.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150429				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>EXPONDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponential distributions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150429				0	gu	DSTDEVP				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3145598				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBSTDABWN\"\>DSTDEVP calculates the standard deviation of a population based on all cells of a data range which match the search criteria.\</ahelp\> The records from the example are treated as the whole population.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149484				0	gu	DSTDEVP(Database; Database Field; Search Criteria)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153322				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155431				0	gu	To find the standard deviation of the weight for all children of the same age at Joe's birthday party (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3143271				0	gu	In row 14, under Age, enter 7, 8, 9, and so on, one after the other. The result is the standard deviation of the weight for all same-aged children whose weight was checked.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154794				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>PV function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>present values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; present values\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
