helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156306	58			0	gu	\<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is the parameter Beta of the Gamma distribution.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147077				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156096				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>EXPONDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponential distributions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156096				0	gu	BETADIST				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147571				0	gu	BETADIST(Number;Alpha;Beta;Start;End)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156107	69			0	gu	\<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is the parameter Beta of the Gamma distribution.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153619	70			0	gu	\<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is the parameter Beta of the Gamma distribution.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145649				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3143228				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>BINOMDIST function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143228				0	gu	BINOMDIST				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146897				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BINOMVERT\"\>Returns the individual term binomial distribution probability.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156009				0	gu	BINOMDIST(X;trials;SP;C)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154304	80			0	gu	\<emph\>X\</emph\> is the number of successes in a set of trials.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147492	81			0	gu	\<emph\>number\</emph\> is the number of repetitions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3146085	82			0	gu	\<emph\>SP\</emph\> is the probability of success for one trial.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149760	83			0	gu	\<emph\>C\</emph\> = 0 calculates the probability of a single event and \<emph\>C\</emph\> = 1 calculates the cumulative probability.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151171				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150120	86			0	gu	=BINOMDIST(A1; 12; 0.5; 1) shows the cumulative probabilities for the same series. For example, if A1 = 4, the cumulative probability of the series is 0, 1, 2, 3 or 4 times \<emph\>Heads\</emph\> (non-exclusive OR).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id0119200902432928				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>CHIINV function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200902421451				0	gu	CHIINV				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902475286				0	gu	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the probability value for which the inverse Gamma distribution is to be calculated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902475282				0	gu	\<emph\>Degrees_freedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom of the experiment.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148835				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>CHIINV function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148835				0	gu	CHIINV				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149906				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHIINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the one-tailed probability of the chi-squared distribution.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150504				0	gu	CHIINV(number; degrees_freedom)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154898	92			0	gu	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value of the error probability.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154294	93			0	gu	\<emph\>Degrees_freedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom of the experiment.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154208				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150777				0	gu	A die is thrown 1020 times. The numbers on the die 1 through 6 come up 195, 151, 148, 189, 183 and 154 times (observation values). The hypothesis that the die is not fixed is to be tested.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3153062				0	gu	The Chi square distribution of the random sample is determined by the formula given above. Since the expected value for a given number on the die for n throws is n times 1/6, thus 1020/6 = 170, the formula returns a Chi square value of 13.27.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148806				0	gu	If the (observed) Chi square is greater than or equal to the (theoretical) Chi square CHIINV, the hypothesis will be discarded, since the deviation between theory and experiment is too great. If the observed Chi square is less that CHIINV, the hypothesis is confirmed with the indicated probability of error.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158401				0	gu	If the probability of error is 5%, the die is not true. If the probability of error is 2%, there is no reason to believe it is fixed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154260				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>CHITEST function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154260				0	gu	CHITEST				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151052				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHITEST\"\>Returns the probability of a deviance from a random distribution of two test series based on the chi-squared test for independence.\</ahelp\> CHITEST returns the chi-squared distribution of the data.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148925				0	gu	The probability determined by CHITEST can also be determined with CHIDIST, in which case the Chi square of the random sample must then be passed as a parameter instead of the data row.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149162				0	gu	CHITEST(Data_B; Data_E)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158421	101			0	gu	\<emph\>Data_B\</emph\> is the array of the observations.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3166453	102			0	gu	\<emph\>Data_E\</emph\> is the range of the expected values.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146946				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154096				0	gu	Data_B (observed)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3152948				0	gu	Data_E (expected)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149481	104			0	gu	=CHITEST(A1:A6; B1:B6) equals 0.02. This is the probability which suffices the observed data of the theoretical Chi-square distribution.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148690				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>CHIDIST function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148690				0	gu	CHIDIST				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156338				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_CHIVERT\"\>Returns the probability value from the indicated Chi square that a hypothesis is confirmed.\</ahelp\> CHIDIST compares the Chi square value to be given for a random sample that is calculated from the sum of (observed value-expected value)^2/expected value for all values with the theoretical Chi square distribution and determines from this the probability of error for the hypothesis to be tested.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3151316				0	gu	The probability determined by CHIDIST can also be determined by CHITEST.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3158439				0	gu	CHIDIST (Number; degrees_freedom)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3148675	110			0	gu	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the chi-square value of the random sample used to determine the error probability.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3155615	111			0	gu	\<emph\>Degrees_freedom\</emph\> are the degrees of freedom of the experiment.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146787				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3156141				0	gu	If the Chi square value of the random sample is 13.27 and if the experiment has 5 degrees of freedom, then the hypothesis is assured with a probability of error of 2%.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id0119200902231887				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>EXPONDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponential distributions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id0119200901583452				0	gu	CHIDIST				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id011920090239564				0	gu	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value for which the F distribution is to be calculated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id0119200902395660				0	gu	\<emph\>Degrees_freedom\</emph\> is the degrees of freedom of the experiment.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150603				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>EXPONDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponential distributions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150603				0	gu	EXPONDIST				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3149563				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_EXPONVERT\"\>Returns the exponential distribution.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3150987				0	gu	EXPONDIST(Number; lambda; C)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154663	119			0	gu	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value of the function.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3154569	120			0	gu	\<emph\>Lambda\</emph\> is the parameter value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	par_id3147332	121			0	gu	\<emph\>C\</emph\> is a logical value that determines the form of the function. \<emph\>C = 0\</emph\> calculates the density function, and \<emph\>C = 1\</emph\> calculates the distribution.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060181.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146133				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12050100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	1st, 2nd, 3rd Group				2002-02-02 02:02:02
