helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159264				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_AMORLINC\"\>Calculates the amount of depreciation for a settlement period as linear amortization. If the capital asset is purchased during the settlement period, the proportional amount of depreciation is considered.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147363				0	gu	AMORLINC(Cost;Date purchased;First period;Salvage;Period;Rate;Basis)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149530	376			0	gu	\<emph\>Salvage\</emph\> is the value of an asset at the end of the depreciation.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150982	378			0	gu	\<emph\>number\</emph\> is the number of repetitions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145257				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>ACCRINT function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145257				0	gu	ACCRINT				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3151276				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_ACCRINT\"\>Calculates the accrued interest of a security in the case of periodic payments.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159092				0	gu	ACCRINT(Issue;First interest;Settlement;Rate;Par;Frequency;Basis)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150519	339			0	gu	\<emph\>Yield\</emph\>: the annual yield of the security.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155376	340			0	gu	\<emph\>First interest\</emph\>: the first interest date of the security.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154486	342			0	gu	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\>: the annual nominal rate of interest (coupon interest rate)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156445	343			0	gu	\<emph\>Yield\</emph\>: the annual yield of the security.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149406	344			0	gu	\<emph\>P\</emph\> is the number of interest payment periods per year.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148699				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148599				0	gu	A security is issued on 2.28.2001. First interest is set for 8.31.2001. The settlement date is 5.1.2001. The Rate is 0.1 or 10% and Par is 1000 currency units. Interest is paid half-yearly (frequency is 2). The basis is the US method (0). How much interest has accrued?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151240				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>EXPONDIST function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponential distributions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151240				0	gu	ACCRINTM				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3157981				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_ACCRINTM\"\>Calculates the accrued interest of a security in the case of one-off payment at the settlement date.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147074				0	gu	ACCRINTM(Issue;Settlement;Rate;Par;Basis)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3144773	352			0	gu	\<emph\>Yield\</emph\>: the annual yield of the security.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153972	354			0	gu	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\>: the annual nominal rate of interest (coupon interest rate).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159204	355			0	gu	\<emph\>Yield\</emph\>: the annual yield of the security.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155384				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154541				0	gu	A security is issued on 4.1.2001. The maturity date is set for 6.15.2001. The Rate is 0.1 or 10% and Par is 1000 currency units. The basis of the daily/annual calculation is the daily balance (3). How much interest has accrued?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145753				0	gu	RECEIVED				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150051				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_RECEIVED\"\>Calculates the amount received that is paid for a fixed-interest security at a given point in time.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145362				0	gu	RECEIVED(Settlement;Maturity;Investment;Discount;Basis)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153011	395			0	gu	Maturity: the date on which the security is sold.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155525	396			0	gu	\<emph\>Investment\</emph\>: the purchase sum.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155760	397			0	gu	\<emph\>Discount\</emph\>: the percentage discount on acquisition of the security.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154710				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154735				0	gu	Settlement date: February 15 1999, maturity date: May 15 1999, investment sum: 1000 currency units, discount: 5.75 per cent, basis: Daily balance/360 = 2.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146108				0	gu	The amount received on the maturity date is calculated as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147556				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>PV function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>present values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; present values\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147556				0	gu	PV				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153301				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_BW\"\>Returns the present value of an investment resulting from a series of regular payments.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146099				0	gu	Use this function to calculate the amount of money needed to be invested at a fixed rate today, to receive a specific amount, an annuity, over a specified number of periods. You can also determine how much money is to remain after the elapse of the period. Specify as well if the amount is to be paid out at the beginning or at the end of each period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153334				0	gu	Enter these values either as numbers, expressions or references. If, for example, interest is paid annually at 8%, but you want to use month as your period, enter 8%/12 under \<emph\>Rate\</emph\> and \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc with automatically calculate the correct factor.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150395				0	gu	PV(Rate; NPER; PMT; FV; Type)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3151341	9			0	gu	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> defines the interest rate per period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153023	10			0	gu	\<emph\>NPER\</emph\> is the total number of periods (payment period).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146323	11			0	gu	\<emph\>PMT\</emph\> is the regular payment made per period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150536	12			0	gu	\<emph\>FV\</emph\> (optional) defines the future value remaining after the final installment has been made.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146883	13			0	gu	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> (optional) denotes due date for payments. Type = 1 means due at the beginning of a period and Type = 0 (default) means due at the end of the period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150037				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145225				0	gu	What is the present value of an investment, if 500 currency units are paid out monthly and the annual interest rate is 8%? The payment period is 48 months and 20,000 currency units are to remain at the end of the payment period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155907	16			0	gu	PV(8%/12;48;500;20000) = -35,019.37 currency units. Under the named conditions, you must deposit 35,019.37 currency units today, if you want to receive 500 currency units per month for 48 months and have 20,000 currency units left over at the end. Cross-checking shows that 48 x 500 currency units + 20,000 currency units = 44,000 currency units. The difference between this amount and the 35,000 currency units deposited represents the interest paid.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149150				0	gu	If you enter references instead of these values into the formula, you can calculate any number of "If-then" scenarios. Please note: references to constants must be defined as absolute references. Examples of this type of application are found under the depreciation functions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152978				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; depreciations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>SYD function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>depreciations; arithmetic declining\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>arithmetic declining depreciations\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152978				0	gu	SYD				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148732				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DIA\"\>Returns the arithmetic-declining depreciation rate.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149886				0	gu	Use this function to calculate the depreciation amount for one period of the total depreciation span of an object. Arithmetic declining depreciation reduces the depreciation amount from period to period by a fixed sum.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150483				0	gu	SYD(Cost;Salvage;Life;Period)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146879	24			0	gu	\<emph\>Cost\</emph\> fixes the initial cost of an asset.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147423	25			0	gu	\<emph\>Salvage\</emph\> is the value of an asset after depreciation.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3151229	26			0	gu	\<emph\>Life\</emph\> is the period fixing the time span over which an asset is depreciated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147473	27			0	gu	\<emph\>Period\</emph\> defines the period for which the depreciation is to be calculated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148434				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149688				0	gu	A video system initially costing 50,000 currency units is to be depreciated annually for the next 5 years. The salvage value is to be 10,000 currency units. You want to calculate depreciation for the first year.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150900	30			0	gu	SYD(50000;10000;5;1)=13,333.33 currency units. The depreciation amount for the first year is 13,333.33 currency units.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146142				0	gu	To have an overview of depreciation rates per period, it is best to define a depreciation table. By entering the different depreciation formulas available in \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc next to each other, you can see which depreciation form is the most appropriate. Enter the table as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149979				0	gu	The formula in E2 is as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156124				0	gu	This formula is duplicated in column E down to E11 (select E2, then drag down the lower right corner with the mouse).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147270				0	gu	Cell E13 contains the formula used to check the total of the depreciation amounts. It uses the SUMIF function as the negative values in E8:E11 must not be considered. The condition >0 is contained in cell A13. The formula in E13 is as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155998				0	gu	Now view the depreciation for a 10 year period, or at a salvage value of 1 currency unit, or enter a different initial cost, and so on.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155104				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;annuities\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>annuities\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PMT function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155104				0	gu	DISC				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153891				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_DISC\"\>Calculates the allowance (discount) of a security as a percentage.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149756				0	gu	DISC(Settlement;Maturity;Price;Redemption;Basis)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154304	384			0	gu	Maturity: the date on which the security is sold.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159180	385			0	gu	\<emph\>Price\</emph\>: The price of the security per 100 currency units of par value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147253	386			0	gu	\<emph\>Redemption\</emph\>: the redemption value of the security per 100 currency units of par value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151174				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155902				0	gu	A security is purchased on 1.25.2001; the maturity date is 11.15.2001. The price (purchase price) is 97, the redemption value is 100. Using daily balance calculation (basis 3) how high is the settlement (discount)?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154695				0	gu	DURATION_ADD				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145768				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_DURATION\"\>Calculates the duration of a fixed interest security in years.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153373				0	gu	DURATION_ADD(Settlement;Maturity;Coupon;Yield;Frequency;Basis)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148558	407			0	gu	Maturity: the date on which the security is sold.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153096	408			0	gu	\<emph\>Coupon\</emph\>: the annual coupon interest rate (nominal rate of interest)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154594	409			0	gu	\<emph\>Yield\</emph\>: the annual yield of the security.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149906	410			0	gu	\<emph\>P\</emph\> is the number of interest payment periods per year.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146995				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148834				0	gu	A security is purchased on 1/1/2001; the maturity date is 1/1/2006. The nominal rate of interest is 8%. The yield is 9.0%. Interest is paid half-yearly (frequency is 2). Using daily balance interest calculation (basis 3) how long is the modified duration?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159147				0	gu	EFFECTIVE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154204				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_EFFEKTIV\"\>Returns the net annual interest rate for a nominal interest rate.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145417				0	gu	Nominal interest refers to the amount of interest due at the end of a calculation period. Effective interest increases with the number of payments made. In other words, interest is often paid in installments (for example, monthly or quarterly) before the end of the calculation period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148805				0	gu	EFFECTIVE(NOM;P)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149768	93			0	gu	\<emph\>NOM\</emph\> is the nominal interest.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149334	94			0	gu	\<emph\>P\</emph\> is the number of interest payment periods per year.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154223				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3144499				0	gu	If the annual nominal interest rate is 9.75% and four interest calculation periods are defined, what is the actual interest rate (effective rate)?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147241				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>Euro; converting in\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>CONVERT function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147241				0	gu	EFFECT_ADD				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147524				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_EFFECT\"\>Calculates the effective annual rate of interest on the basis of the nominal interest rate and the number of interest payments per annum.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155118				0	gu	EFFECT_ADD(Nominal rate;Npery)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154274	419			0	gu	\<emph\>P\</emph\> is the number of interest payment periods per year.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149156				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3158426				0	gu	What is the effective annual rate of interest for a 5.25% nominal rate and quarterly payment.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149998				0	gu	DDB				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159190				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GDA\"\>Returns the depreciation of an asset for a specified period using the arithmetic-declining method.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152361				0	gu	Use this form of depreciation if you require a higher initial depreciation value as opposed to linear depreciation. The depreciation value gets less with each period and is usually used for assets whose value loss is higher shortly after purchase (for example, vehicles, computers). Please note that the book value will never reach zero under this calculation type.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3166452				0	gu	DDB(Cost;Salvage;Life;Period;Factor)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153237	104			0	gu	\<emph\>Cost\</emph\> fixes the initial cost of an asset.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149787	105			0	gu	\<emph\>Salvage\</emph\> fixes the value of an asset at the end of its life.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149736	107			0	gu	\<emph\>Period\</emph\> defines the period for which the depreciation is to be calculated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150243	108			0	gu	\<emph\>Factor\</emph\> (optional) is the factor by which depreciation decreases. If a value is not entered, the default is factor 2.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159274				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152882				0	gu	A computer system with an initial cost of 75,000 currency units is to be depreciated monthly over 5 years. The value at the end of the depreciation is to be 1 currency unit. The factor is 2.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154106	111			0	gu	DDB(75000;1;60;12;2) = 1,721.81 currency units. Therefore, the double-declining depreciation during the first month after purchase is 1,721.81 currency units.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149962				0	gu	DB				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148989				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GDA2\"\>Returns the depreciation of an asset for a specified period using the double-declining balance method.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156213				0	gu	This form of depreciation is used if you want to get a higher depreciation value at the beginning of the depreciation (as opposed to linear depreciation). The depreciation value is reduced with every depreciation period by the depreciation already deducted from the initial cost.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153349				0	gu	DB(Cost;Salvage;Life;Period;Month)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148462	118			0	gu	\<emph\>Cost\</emph\> fixes the initial cost of an asset.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148658	119			0	gu	\<emph\>Salvage\</emph\> is the value of an asset at the end of the depreciation.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145371	120			0	gu	\<emph\>Life\</emph\> Life defines the period over which an asset is depreciated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154608	121			0	gu	\<emph\>Period\</emph\> is the length of each period. The length must be entered in the same date unit as the depreciation period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150829	122			0	gu	\<emph\>Month\</emph\> (optional) denotes the number of months for the first year of depreciation. If an entry is not defined, 12 is used as the default.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151130				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156147				0	gu	A computer system with an initial cost of 25,000 currency units is to be depreciated over a three year period. The salvage value is to be 1,000 currency units. One period is 30 days.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159242				0	gu	The fixed-declining depreciation of the computer system is 1,075.00 currency units.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153948				0	gu	IRR				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3143282				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_IKV\"\>Calculates the internal rate of return for an investment.\</ahelp\> The values represent cash flow values at regular intervals, at least one value must be negative (payments), and at least one value must be positive (income).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155427				0	gu	IRR(Values;Guess)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3144758	132			0	gu	\<emph\>Values\</emph\> represents an array containing the values.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149233	133			0	gu	\<emph\>Guess\</emph\> (optional) is the estimated value. An iterative method is used to calculate the internal rate of return. If you can provide only few values, you should provide an initial guess to enable the iteration.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151258				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151012				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; interests for unchanged amortization installments\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>interests for unchanged amortization installments\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>ISPMT function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151012				0	gu	ISPMT				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148693				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ISPMT\"\>Calculates the level of interest for unchanged amortization installments.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146070				0	gu	ISPMT(Rate; Period; Total_periods; Invest)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148672	318			0	gu	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> sets the periodic interest rate.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145777	319			0	gu	\<emph\>Period\</emph\> is the number of installments for calculation of interest.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153678	320			0	gu	\<emph\>Total_periods\</emph\> is the total number of installment periods.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159390	321			0	gu	\<emph\>Invest\</emph\> is the amount of the investment.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156162				0	gu	Examples 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149558				0	gu	For a credit amount of 120,000 currency units with a two-year term and monthly installments, at a yearly interest rate of 12% the level of interest after 1.5 years is required.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150949	324			0	gu	ISPMT(1%;18;24;120000) = -300 currency units. The monthly interest after 1.5 years amounts to 300 currency units.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146812				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060119.xhp\" name=\"Forward to Financial Functions Part Two\"\>Financial Functions Part Two\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
