helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\main.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166464				0	gu	પરચૂરણ				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\integer_leading_zero.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Entering a Number with Leading Zeros				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\integer_leading_zero.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147560				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>zero values; entering leading zeros\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>numbers; with leading zeros\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>leading zeros\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>integers with leading zeros\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>numbers; changing text/number formats\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; changing text/number formats\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formats; changing text/number\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>text in cells; changing to numbers\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\integer_leading_zero.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147560				0	gu	\<variable id=\"integer_leading_zero\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/integer_leading_zero.xhp\" name=\"Entering a Number with Leading Zeros\"\>Entering a Number with Leading Zeros\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\integer_leading_zero.xhp	0	help	par_id3153194				0	gu	There are various ways to enter integers starting with a zero:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\integer_leading_zero.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	gu	Enter the number as text. The easiest way is to enter the number starting with an apostrophe (for example, '0987). The apostrophe will not appear in the cell, and the number will be formatted as text. Because it is in text format, however, you cannot calculate with this number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\integer_leading_zero.xhp	0	help	par_id3154013				0	gu	Format a cell with a number format such as \\0000. This format can be assigned in the \<emph\>Format code\</emph\> field under the \<emph\>Format - Cells - Numbers\</emph\> tab, and defines the cell display as "always put a zero first and then the integer, having at least three places, and filled with zeros at the left if less than three digits".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\integer_leading_zero.xhp	0	help	par_id3153158				0	gu	If you want to apply a numerical format to a column of numbers in text format (for example, text "000123" becomes number "123"), do the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\integer_leading_zero.xhp	0	help	par_id3149377				0	gu	Select the column in which the digits are found in text format. Set the cell format in that column as "Number".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Applying Goal Seek				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145068				0	gu	\<variable id=\"goalseek\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/goalseek.xhp\" name=\"Applying Goal Seek\"\>Applying Goal Seek\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171				0	gu	With the help of Goal Seek you can calculate a value that, as part of a formula, leads to the result you specify for the formula. You thus define the formula with several fixed values and one variable value and the result of the formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153966				0	gu	Goal Seek Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3150871				0	gu	To calculate annual interest (I), create a table with the values for the capital (C), number of years (n), and interest rate (i). The formula is:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3152596				0	gu	I = C * n* i				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335				0	gu	Let us assume that the interest rate \<item type=\"literal\"\>i\</item\> of 7.5% and the number of years \<item type=\"literal\"\>n\</item\> (1) will remain constant. However, you want to know how much the investment capital \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\> would have to be modified in order to attain a particular return \<item type=\"literal\"\>I\</item\>. For this example, calculate how much capital \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\> would be required if you want an annual return of $15,000.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3155960				0	gu	Enter each of the values for Capital \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\> (an arbitrary value like \<item type=\"literal\"\>$100,000\</item\>), number of years \<item type=\"literal\"\>n \</item\>(\<item type=\"literal\"\>1\</item\>), and interest rate \<item type=\"literal\"\>i\</item\> (\<item type=\"literal\"\>7.5%\</item\>) in one cell each. Enter the formula to calculate the interest \<item type=\"literal\"\>I\</item\> in another cell. Instead of \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\>, \<item type=\"literal\"\>n\</item\>, and \<item type=\"literal\"\>i\</item\> use the \<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/relativ_absolut_ref.xhp\"\>reference to the cell\</link\> with the corresponding value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3147001				0	gu	Place the cursor in the cell containing the interest \<item type=\"literal\"\>I\</item\>, and choose \<emph\>Tools - Goal Seek\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Goal Seek\</emph\> dialog appears.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088				0	gu	The correct cell is already entered in the field \<emph\>Formula Cell\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3166426				0	gu	Place the cursor in the field \<emph\>Variable Cell\</emph\>. In the sheet, click in the cell that contains the value to be changed, in this example it is the cell with the capital value \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3150369				0	gu	Enter the expected result of the formula in the \<emph\>Target Value\</emph\> text box. In this example, the value is 15,000. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3146978				0	gu	A dialog appears informing you that the Goal Seek was successful. Click \<emph\>Yes\</emph\> to enter the result in the cell with the variable value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3149409				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/06040000.xhp\" name=\"Goal Seek\"\>Goal Seek\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Calculating With Formulas				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155411				0	gu	\<variable id=\"formulas\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/formulas.xhp\" name=\"Calculating With Formulas\"\>Calculating With Formulas\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272				0	gu	Remember that the basic arithmetic signs (+, -, *, /) can be used in formulas using the "Multiplication and Division before Addition and Subtraction" rule. Instead of writing =SUM(A1:B1) it is better to write =A1+B1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	gu	Parentheses can also be used. The result of the formula =(1+2)*3 produces a different result than =1+2*3.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3156285				0	gu	Here are a few examples of $[officename] Calc formulas:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3154015				0	gu	=A1+10				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3146972				0	gu	Displays the contents of cell A1 plus 10.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3145643				0	gu	=A1*16%				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3154255				0	gu	Displays 16% of the contents of A1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3146917				0	gu	=A1 * A2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3146315				0	gu	Displays the result of the multiplication of A1 and A2.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3154022				0	gu	=ROUND(A1;1)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3150209				0	gu	=EFFECTIVE(5%;12)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3150883				0	gu	Calculates the effective interest for 5% annual nominal interest with 12 payments a year.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3146114				0	gu	=B8-SUM(B10:B14)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3154486				0	gu	Calculates B8 minus the sum of the cells B10 to B14.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3152890				0	gu	=SUM(B8;SUM(B10:B14))				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3159171				0	gu	Calculates the sum of cells B10 to B14 and adds the value to B8.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3150109				0	gu	It is also possible to nest functions in formulas, as shown in the example. You can also nest functions within functions. The Function Wizard assists you with nested functions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3150213				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060100.xhp\" name=\"Functions list\"\>Functions list\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formulas.xhp	0	help	par_id3152869				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060000.xhp\" name=\"AutoPilot: Functions\"\>Function Wizard\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Displaying Formulas or Values				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153195				0	gu	\<variable id=\"formula_value\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/formula_value.xhp\" name=\"Displaying Formulas or Values\"\>Displaying Formulas or Values\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010				0	gu	If you want to display the formulas in the cells, for example in the form =SUM(A1:B5), proceed as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120				0	gu	In the \<emph\>Display\</emph\> area mark the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> box. Click OK.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3147396				0	gu	If you want to view the calculation results instead of the formula, do not mark the Formulasbox.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Calculating in Spreadsheets				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150791				0	gu	\<variable id=\"calculate\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calculate.xhp\" name=\"Calculating in Spreadsheets\"\>Calculating in Spreadsheets\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120				0	gu	The following is an example of a calculation in $[officename] Calc.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951				0	gu	Click in a cell, and type a number				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1065D				0	gu	The cursor moves down to the next cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064				0	gu	Enter another number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN10676				0	gu	The cursor moves to the right into the next cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253				0	gu	Type in a formula, for example, \<item type=\"literal\"\>=A3 * A4 / 100.\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343				0	gu	The result of the formula appears in the cell. If you want, you can edit the formula in the input line of the Formula bar.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155378				0	gu	When you edit a formula, the new result is calculated automatically.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Defining Database Ranges				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154758				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>tables; database ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>database ranges; defining\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>ranges; defining database ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining;database ranges\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154758				0	gu	\<variable id=\"database_define\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/database_define.xhp\" name=\"Defining Database Ranges\"\>Defining a Database Range\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768				0	gu	You can define a range of cells in a spreadsheet to use as a database. Each row in this database range corresponds to a database record and each cell in a row corresponds to a database field. You can sort, group, search, and perform calculations on the range as you would in a database.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145801	82			0	gu	You can only edit and access a database range in the spreadsheet that contains the range. You cannot access the database range in the %PRODUCTNAME Data Sources view. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10648				0	gu	To Define a Database Range				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064				0	gu	Select the range of cells that you want to define as a database range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10654				0	gu	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Define Range\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3153715				0	gu	In the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> box, enter a name for the database range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066A				0	gu	Click \<emph\>More\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253				0	gu	Specify the options for the database range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10675				0	gu	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_grouping.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066B				0	gu	Choose \<emph\>Data - Outline - Group\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_grouping.xhp	0	help	par_idN10682				0	gu	To remove a grouping, click inside the group, then choose \<emph\>Data - Outline - Ungroup\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Navigating Through Sheets Tabs				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>sheets; showing multiple\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheet tabs;using\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>views;multiple sheets\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769	4			0	gu	\<variable id=\"multi_tables\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/multi_tables.xhp\" name=\"Navigating Through Sheets Tabs\"\>Navigating Through Sheets Tabs\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	par_id3153771				0	gu	By default $[officename] displays three sheets "Sheet1" to "Sheet3", in each new spreadsheet. You can switch between sheets in a spreadsheet using the sheet tabs at the bottom of the screen.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	par_idN106AF				0	gu	\<image id=\"img_id4829822\" src=\"res/helpimg/sheettabs.png\" width=\"84.67mm\" height=\"19.4mm\" localize=\"true\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id4829822\"\>Sheet Tabs\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	par_id3153144				0	gu	\<image id=\"img_id3156441\" src=\"res/helpimg/calcnav.png\" width=\"1.667cm\" height=\"0.37cm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3156441\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	par_id3147396				0	gu	Use the navigation buttons to display all the sheets belonging to your document. Clicking the button on the far left or the far right displays, respectively, the first or last sheet tab. The middle buttons allow the user to scroll forward and backward through all sheet tabs. To display the sheet itself click on the sheet tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multi_tables.xhp	0	help	par_id3149379				0	gu	If there is insufficient space to display all the sheet tabs, you can increase it by pointing to the separator between the scrollbar and the sheet tabs, pressing the mouse button and, keeping the mouse button pressed, dragging to the right. In doing so you will be sharing the available space between the sheet tabs and horizontal scrollbar.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Formatting Numbers With Decimals				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145367				0	gu	\<variable id=\"format_value\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/format_value.xhp\" name=\"Formatting Numbers With Decimals\"\>Formatting Numbers With Decimals\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576				0	gu	Enter a number into the sheet, for example, 1234.5678. This number will be displayed in the default number format, with two decimal places. You will see 1234.57 when you confirm the entry. Only the display in the document will be rounded off; internally, the number retains all four decimal places after the decimal point.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3154012				0	gu	To format numbers with decimals:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3147394				0	gu	Set the cursor at the number and choose \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\> to start the \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3153157				0	gu	On the \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab you will see a selection of predefined number formats. In the bottom right in the dialog you will see a preview of how your current number would look if you were to give it a particular format.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3155766				0	gu	\<image id=\"img_id3149021\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_numberformatincdecimals.png\" width=\"5.64mm\" height=\"5.64mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3149021\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3149256				0	gu	If you only want to modify the number of the decimal places displayed, the easiest method is to use the \<emph\>Number Format: Add Decimal Place\</emph\> or \<emph\>Number Format: Delete Decimal Place\</emph\> icons on the Formatting Bar.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150767				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>database ranges; sorting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sorting; database ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data;sorting in databases\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150767				0	gu	\<variable id=\"database_sort\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/database_sort.xhp\" name=\"Sorting Database Ranges\"\>Sorting Database Ranges\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	par_id3145751				0	gu	Click in a database range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	par_idN10635				0	gu	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Sort\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	par_idN10645				0	gu	Select the sort options that you want.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	par_idN1063D				0	gu	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Entering Matrix Formulas				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153969				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>matrices; entering matrix formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; matrix formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;matrix formulas\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153969				0	gu	\<variable id=\"matrixformula\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/matrixformula.xhp\" name=\"Entering Matrix Formulas\"\>Entering Matrix Formulas\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153144				0	gu	The following is an example of how you can enter a matrix formula, without going into the details of matrix functions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153188				0	gu	Assume you have entered 10 numbers in Columns A and B (A1:A10 and B1:B10), and would like to calculate the sum of each row in Column C.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3154321				0	gu	Using the mouse, select the range C1:C10, in which the results are to be displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149260				0	gu	Press F2, or click in the input line of the Formula bar.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944				0	gu	Enter an equal sign (=).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3145252				0	gu	Select the range A1:A10, which contains the first values for the sum formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3144767				0	gu	Press the (+) key from the numerical keypad.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3154018				0	gu	Select the numbers in the second column in cells B1:B10.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716				0	gu	End the input with the matrix key combination: Shift+\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Enter.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	gu	The matrix area is automatically protected against modifications, such as deleting rows or columns. It is, however, possible to edit any formatting, such as the cell background.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Using Scenarios				2002-02-02 02:02:02
