helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05030000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147584				0	gu	નીચે				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05030000.xhp	0	help	par_id3148746				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_LOWERBORDER_DIST\"\>The bottom border is positioned between the formula and background.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05030000.xhp	0	help	par_id3155143				0	gu	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:PUSHBUTTON:DLG_DEFINE_CUSTOMSHOW:BTN_ADD\"\>Saves your changes as your default settings for all new formulas.\</ahelp\> A security response will appear before saving these changes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Brackets and Grouping				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147341				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>brackets and grouping in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>grouping and brackets in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147341				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091100.xhp\" name=\"Brackets and Grouping\"\>Brackets and Grouping\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150342				0	gu	Note: the quotation marks in the examples are used to emphasize text and do not belong to the content of the formulas and commands.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146962				0	gu	When typing example formulas into the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window, note that spaces are often required for correct structure.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149054				0	gu	Braces "{}" are used to group expressions together to form one new expression. For example, "sqrt {x * y}" is the square root of the entire product x*y, while "sqrt x * y" is the square root of x multiplied by y. Braces do not require an extra space.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147403				0	gu	There are a total of eight (8) different types of brackets available. The "ceil" and "floor" brackets are often used for rounding up or down the argument to the next integer: "lceil -3.7 rceil = -3" or "lfloor -3.7 rfloor = -4".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146320				0	gu	Operator brackets, also known as Bra-kets (angle brackets with a vertical line in between), are common in Physics notation: "langle a mline b rangle" or "langle a mline b mline c over d mline e rangle." The height and positioning of the vertical lines always corresponds exactly to the enclosing brackets.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157870				0	gu	All brackets may only be used in pairs. The brackets have some common characteristics:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155761				0	gu	All types of brackets have the same grouping function as described for "{}"\<emph\>-\</emph\> brackets.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145590				0	gu	All types of brackets, including those that are visible, permit empty group definition. The enclosed expression may therefore be empty.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154562				0	gu	Brackets do not adjust their size to the enclosed expression. For example, if you want "( a over b )" with a bracket size adjusted to a and b you must insert "left" and "right". Entering "left(a over b right)" produces appropriate sizing. If, however, the brackets themselves are part of the expression whose size is changed, they are included the size change: "size 3(a over b)" and "size 12(a over b)". The sizing of the bracket-to-bracket expression ratio does not change in any way.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153002				0	gu	Since "left" and "right" ensure unique assignment of the brackets, every single bracket can be used as an argument for these two commands, even placing right brackets on the left side, or left brackets on the right. Instead of a bracket you can use the "none" qualifier, which means that there is no bracket shown and that there is no space reserved for a bracket. Using this, you can create the following expressions:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150014				0	gu	left lbrace x right none				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149877				0	gu	left [ x right )				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145241				0	gu	left ] x right [				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156060				0	gu	left rangle x right lfloor				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150935				0	gu	The same rules apply to "left" and "right" as to the other brackets: they also work as group builders and may enclose empty expressions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149030				0	gu	The combination of mismatched brackets, single brackets and repositioned left and right brackets occurs often in mathematical formulas. The following is a formula that will create an error when typed:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155989				0	gu	[2, 3) - right open interval				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153720				0	gu	The complete overview is as follows				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150756				0	gu	\\(, \\)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145207				0	gu	\\[, \\]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147514				0	gu	In this way, intervals like the one above can be built in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> without any problems: \\[2", "3\\) or "\\]2", "3\\[ (Attention: These quotation marks are part of the entry.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153532				0	gu	Please note that the quotation marks must be entered and can be obtained with \<emph\>Shift+2\</emph\> and not with typographical quotation marks. Generally, punctuation marks (like the comma in this case) are set as text. Although it is also possible to type "\\[2,~3\\)" the above option is preferable. In the previous example, "fixed size" always describes a bracket size dependent on the font size used.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153674				0	gu	Nesting groups within each other is relatively problem-free. In the formula hat "{a + b}" the "hat" is displayed simply over the center of "{a + b}". Also, "color red lceil a rceil" and "grave hat langle x * y rangle" work as expected. The result of the latter can be compared to "grave {hat langle x * y rangle}." These attributes do not compete, but rather can be combined.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147526				0	gu	This differs slightly for competing or mutually influencing attributes. This is often the case with font attributes. For example, which color does the b have in "color yellow color red (a + color green b)", or which size does it have in "size *4 (a + size /2 b)"? Given a base size of 12, does it have the size 48, 6 or even 24 (which could be seen as a combination)? The following are basic resolution rules, which will be followed consistently in the future. In general, the rules apply to all group operations. This only has a visible effect on the font attributes, like "bold", "ital", "phantom", "size", "color" and "font":				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152952				0	gu	Group operations in sequence are treated as if every single operation is enclosed by braces. They are nested, and in every level there can be no more than one operation. Here is an example of a formula with many group operations: "size 12 color red font sans size -5 (a + size 8 b)" like "{size 12{color red{font sans{size -5 (a + {size 8 b})}}}}".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158441				0	gu	This example formula is then interpreted from left to right. The operations only affect its corresponding group (or expression). Operations further to the right "replace" or "combine themselves with" their predecessors.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150994				0	gu	A group operation does not have any effect on higher-level operations but rather affects only lower-level groups and expressions, including their brackets and super-/subscripts. For example, "a + size *2 (b * size -8 c_1)^2"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154196				0	gu	"color ..." and "font ..." as well as "size n" (n is a decimal) replace any preceding operations of the same type				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154136				0	gu	for "size +n", "size -n", "size *n", and "size /n" the effects of the operations are combined,				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146934				0	gu	"size *2 size -5 a" would be double the starting size minus 5				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149297				0	gu	"font sans ( a + font serif b)"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155174				0	gu	"size *2 ( a + size /2 b )"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154906				0	gu	To change the size of a formula, use "size +" or -,*,/. Do not use "size n". These can easily be used in any context. This enables you to copy to other areas by using Copy and Paste, and the result remains the same. Furthermore, such expressions survive a change of base size in the menu better than when using "size n". If you use only "size *" and "size /" (for example, "size *1.24 a or size /0.86 a") the proportions remain intact.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147587				0	gu	Examples (with a base size of 12 and 50% for indexes):				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148734				0	gu	Exactly identical proportions with "size 18 a_n" and "size *1.5 a_n".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152766				0	gu	This differs in different contexts: "x^{size 18 a_n}" and "x^{size *1.5 a_n}"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157986				0	gu	Examples with size +n for a comparison. They look identical:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158001				0	gu	a_{size 8 n}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147332				0	gu	a_{size +2 n}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155143				0	gu	a_{size *1.333 n}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147129				0	gu	The following examples, however, do not look identical:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147073				0	gu	x^{a_{size 8 n}}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147086				0	gu	x^{a_{size +2 n}}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154386				0	gu	x^{a_{size *1.333 n}}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153354				0	gu	Note that all n here have different sizes. The size 1.333 results from 8/6, the desired size divided by the default index size 6. (Index size 50% with a base size of 12)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090908.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	વર્ગમૂળ				2002-02-02 02:02:02
