helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Controlling Program Execution				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145136				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03090000.xhp\" name=\"Controlling Program Execution\"\>Controlling Program Execution\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090000.xhp	0	help	par_id3143268				0	gu	The following statements control the execution of a program.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156152				0	gu	A program generally executes from the first line of code to the last line of code. You can also execute certain procedures within the program according to specific conditions, or repeat a section of the program within a sub-procedure or function. You can use loops to repeat parts of a program as many times as necessary, or until a certain condition is met. These type of control statements are classified as Condition, Loop, or Jump statements.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
