helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149456				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020300.xhp\"\>Using Procedures and Functions\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767				0	gu	The following describes the basic use of procedures and functions in $[officename] Basic.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151215				0	gu	When you create a new module, $[officename] Basic automatically inserts a SUB called "Main". This default name has nothing to do with the order or the starting point of a $[officename] Basic project. You can also safely rename this SUB.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154124				0	gu	Procedures (SUBS) and functions (FUNCTIONS) help you maintaining a structured overview by separating a program into logical pieces.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193				0	gu	One benefit of procedures and functions is that, once you have developed a program code containing task components, you can use this code in another project.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770				0	gu	Passing Variables to Procedures (SUB) and Functions (FUNCTION)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155414				0	gu	Variables can be passed to both procedures and functions. The SUB or FUNCTION must be declared to expect parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710				0	gu	SUB SubName(\<emph\>Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...\</emph\>)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
