helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Format Function [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153539				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>Format function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153539				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03120301.xhp\" name=\"Format Function [Runtime]\"\>Format Function [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156042				0	gu	Converts a number to a string, and then formats it according to the format that you specify.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153527				0	gu	Format (Number [, Format As String])				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149178				0	gu	Return values:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148474				0	gu	Strings				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149415				0	gu	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Numeric expression that you want to convert to a formatted string.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147531				0	gu	\<emph\>Format:\</emph\> String that specifies the format code for the number. If \<emph\>Format\</emph\> is omitted, the Format function works like the \<emph\>Str\</emph\> function.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147561				0	gu	Formatting Codes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147265				0	gu	The following list describes the codes that you can use for formatting a number:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153380				0	gu	\<emph\>0:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has a digit at the position of the 0 in the format code, the digit is displayed, otherwise a zero is displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151210				0	gu	If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has fewer digits than the number of zeros in the format code, (on either side of the decimal), leading or trailing zeros are displayed. If the number has more digits to the left of the decimal separator than the amount of zeros in the format code, the additional digits are displayed without formatting.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151176	14			0	gu	Decimal places in the number are rounded according to the number of zeros that appear after the decimal separator in the \<emph\>Format \</emph\>code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154123				0	gu	\<emph\>#:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> contains a digit at the position of the # placeholder in the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> code, the digit is displayed, otherwise nothing is displayed at this position.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148452	16			0	gu	This symbol works like the 0, except that leading or trailing zeroes are not displayed if there are more # characters in the format code than digits in the number. Only the relevant digits of the number are displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159150				0	gu	\<emph\>.:\</emph\> The decimal placeholder determines the number of decimal places to the left and right of the decimal separator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159252				0	gu	If the format code contains only # placeholders to the left of this symbol, numbers less than 1 begin with a decimal separator. To always display a leading zero with fractional numbers, use 0 as a placeholder for the first digit to the left of the decimal separator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	gu	\<emph\>%:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and inserts the percent sign (%) where the number appears in the format code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149481				0	gu	\<emph\>E- E+ e- e+ :\</emph\> If the format code contains at least one digit placeholder (0 or #) to the right of the symbol E-, E+, e-, or e+, the number is formatted in the scientific or exponential format. The letter E or e is inserted between the number and the exponent. The number of placeholders for digits to the right of the symbol determines the number of digits in the exponent.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262				0	gu	If the exponent is negative, a minus sign is displayed directly before an exponent with E-, E+, e-, e+. If the exponent is positive, a plus sign is only displayed before exponents with E+ or e+.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148617				0	gu	The thousands delimiter is displayed if the format code contains the delimiter enclosed by digit placeholders (0 or #).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3163713				0	gu	The use of a period as a thousands and decimal separator is dependent on the regional setting. When you enter a number directly in Basic source code, always use a period as decimal delimiter. The actual character displayed as a decimal separator depends on the number format in your system settings.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152887				0	gu	\<emph\>- + $ ( ) space:\</emph\> A plus (+), minus (-), dollar ($), space, or brackets entered directly in the format code is displayed as a literal character.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576				0	gu	To display characters other than the ones listed here, you must precede it by a backslash (\\), or enclose it in quotation marks (" ").				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153139				0	gu	\\ : The backslash displays the next character in the format code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153366				0	gu	Characters in the format code that have a special meaning can only be displayed as literal characters if they are preceded by a backslash. The backslash itself is not displayed, unless you enter a double backslash (\\\\) in the format code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155411				0	gu	Characters that must be preceded by a backslash in the format code in order to be displayed as literal characters are date- and time-formatting characters (a, c, d, h, m, n, p, q, s, t, w, y, /, :), numeric-formatting characters (#, 0, %, E, e, comma, period), and string-formatting characters (@, &, <, >, !).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749				0	gu	You can also use the following predefined number formats. Except for "General Number", all of the predefined format codes return the number as a decimal number with two decimal places.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150113				0	gu	If you use predefined formats, the name of the format must be enclosed in quotation marks.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149377				0	gu	Predefined format				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154730				0	gu	\<emph\>General Number:\</emph\> Numbers are displayed as entered.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153158				0	gu	\<emph\>Currency:\</emph\> Inserts a dollar sign in front of the number and encloses negative numbers in brackets.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490				0	gu	\<emph\>Fixed:\</emph\> Displays at least one digit in front of the decimal separator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415				0	gu	\<emph\>Standard:\</emph\> Displays numbers with a thousands separator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715				0	gu	\<emph\>Percent:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and appends a percent sign to the number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153836				0	gu	\<emph\>Scientific:\</emph\> Displays numbers in scientific format (for example, 1.00E+03 for 1000).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707				0	gu	A format code can be divided into three sections that are separated by semicolons. The first part defines the format for positive values, the second part for negative values, and the third part for zero. If you only specify one format code, it applies to all numbers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156054				0	gu	Sub ExampleFormat				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148993				0	gu	MsgBox Format(6328.2, "##,##0.00")				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A2				0	gu	REM always use a period as decimal delimiter when you enter numbers in Basic source code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147339				0	gu	REM displays for example 6,328.20 in English locale, 6.328,20 in German locale.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	tit				0	gu	Dir Function [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154347				0	gu	\<bookmark_value\>Dir function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154347				0	gu	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03020404.xhp\" name=\"Dir Function [Runtime]\"\>Dir Function [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153381				0	gu	Returns the name of a file, a directory, or all of the files and the directories on a drive or in a directory that match the specified search path.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3156282				0	gu	Dir [(Text As String) [, Attrib As Integer]]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156424				0	gu	Return values:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193				0	gu	Strings				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3161831				0	gu	\<emph\>Text:\</emph\> Any string expression that specifies the search path, directory or file. This argument can only be specified the first time that you call the Dir function. If you want, you can enter the path in \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/00000002.xhp\" name=\"URL notation\"\>URL notation\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3146974	9			0	gu	\<emph\>Attrib: \</emph\>Any integer expression that specifies bitwise file attributes. The Dir function only returns files or directories that match the specified attributes. You can combine several attributes by adding the attribute values:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153952				0	gu	Use this attribute to check if a file or directory exists, or to determine all files and folders in a specific directory.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3159156				0	gu	To check if a file exists, enter the complete path and name of the file. If the file or directory name does not exist, the Dir function returns a zero-length string ("").				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3154012	18			0	gu	To generate a list of all existing files in a specific directory, proceed as follows: The first time you call the Dir function, specify the complete search path for the files, for example, "D:\\Files\\*.sxw". If the path is correct and the search finds at least one file, the Dir function returns the name of the first file that matches the search path. To return additional file names that match the path, call Dir again, but with no arguments.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3147348				0	gu	To return directories only, use the attribute parameter. The same applies if you want to determine the name of a volume (for example, a hard drive partition)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3147125				0	gu	Sub ExampleDir				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3148455				0	gu	REM Displays all files and directories				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3147396				0	gu	Dim sDay As String				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3149378				0	gu	Dim sDir as String, sValue as String				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153416				0	gu	sDir="Directories:"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153838				0	gu	sPath = CurDir				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327				0	gu	sValue = Dir$(sPath + getPathSeparator + "*",16)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153764				0	gu	If sValue <> "." and sValue <> ".." Then				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3155766				0	gu	if (GetAttr( sPath + getPathSeparator + sValue) AND 16) >0 then				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253				0	gu	REM get the directories				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3159264				0	gu	sDir = sDir & chr(13) & sValue				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3145148				0	gu	EndIf				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_idN10700				0	gu	EndIf				2002-02-02 02:02:02
