helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3150358				0	gu	\<emph\>Counter:\</emph\> Loop counter initially assigned the value to the right of the equal sign (start). Only numeric variables are valid. The loop counter increases or decreases according to the variable Step until End is passed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3152455				0	gu	\<emph\>Start:\</emph\> Numeric variable that defines the initial value at the beginning of the loop.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3151043				0	gu	\<emph\>End:\</emph\> Numeric variable that defines the final value at the end of the loop.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3156281				0	gu	\<emph\>Step:\</emph\> Sets the value by which to increase or decrease the loop counter. If Step is not specified, the loop counter is incremented by 1. In this case, End must be greater than Start. If you want to decrease Counter, End must be less than Start, and Step must be assigned a negative value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3154684				0	gu	The \<emph\>For...Next\</emph\> loop repeats all of the statements in the loop for the number of times that is specified by the parameters.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3147287				0	gu	As the counter variable is decreased, $[officename] Basic checks if the end value has been reached. As soon as the counter passes the end value, the loop automatically ends.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154				0	gu	It is possible to nest \<emph\>For...Next\</emph\> statements. If you do not specify a variable following the \<emph\>Next\</emph\> statement, \<emph\>Next\</emph\> automatically refers to the most recent \<emph\>For\</emph\> statement.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3155306				0	gu	If you specify an increment of 0, the statements between \<emph\>For\</emph\> and \<emph\>Next\</emph\> are repeated continuously.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3155854				0	gu	When counting down the counter variable, $[officename] Basic checks for overflow or underflow. The loop ends when Counter exceeds End (positive Step value) or is less than End (negative Step value).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3145273				0	gu	Use the \<emph\>Exit For\</emph\> statement to exit the loop unconditionally. This statement must be within a \<emph\>For...Next\</emph\> loop. Use the \<emph\>If...Then\</emph\> statement to test the exit condition as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3153190				0	gu	For...				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3149482				0	gu	Statement				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3147124				0	gu	If condition = True Then Exit For				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3153159				0	gu	Statement				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3154096				0	gu	આગળ				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3156286				0	gu	Note: In nested \<emph\>For...Next\</emph\> loops, if you exit a loop unconditionally with \<emph\>Exit For\</emph\>, only one loop is exited.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
